{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If you walk in My statutes.</b> Statutes are royal decrees by which a person conducts the affairs of his life, and conducting oneself in them is called walking, as it says: וּבְחֻקֹּתֵיהֶם לֹא תֵלֵכוּ (Leviticus 18:3), and similarly וְאֶת חֻקֹּתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ לָלֶכֶת בָּהֶם (Leviticus 18:4), and similarly בְּחֻקֹּת הַחַיִּים הָלָךְ (Ezekiel 33:15)." "<b>And you shall keep My commandments.</b> Keeping the commandments means attentiveness to how they are performed and to their intent; this requires proper study, as the Sages said: שָׁמוֹר זוֹ מִשְׁנָה (i.e., 'keep' refers to study). He said: if so — if you conduct yourselves in the ways of the blessed El that are included in the practical portion of His Torah, and you study the commandments to know how to perform them and their end, then through this you fulfill His intent that you be in His image, after His likeness." "<b>And you shall do them.</b> Then this perfection will be yours in actual practice, such that you do them as agents who act out of the soul's desire, not as ones commanded who act from fear — in which case the doer is in some respect acted upon — but rather you shall do them out of love, desiring the will of the blessed El, as they said (Avot): make His will as your will."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:38:10.405147374Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}